WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.850
My pupils drew our

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.690
rainfall as a pie chart.

3
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.250
Twelve slices, one for each month.

4
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.270
Nobody could see when the rains came.

5
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.460
The data was right, but

6
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.740
the chart was wrong.

7
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.380
Today: which chart fits which

8
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.100
question, and how charts can mislead.

9
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.130
A bar chart compares groups.

10
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.380
Which fruit does our class like best?

11
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.630
One bar for each fruit, and

12
00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:30.790
the tallest bar wins.

13
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.340
Mango wins easily here,

14
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.740
with eleven pupils.

15
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.660
A line chart shows change over time.

16
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.920
Rainfall month by month makes a line that

17
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.600
rises in the rainy season and falls again.

18
00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.430
Now everyone can see when the rains come.

19
00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.190
A pie chart shows how one whole is

20
00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.290
shared out, like the school budget:

21
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.650
books, repairs, sports and food.

22
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.520
Use it only with a few slices

23
00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.240
that add up to the whole.

24
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.130
A scatter chart puts two measurements of

25
00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.470
each pupil on one picture, like hours of

26
00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.050
reading and spelling marks.

27
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.400
Each dot is one pupil.

28
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.540
If the dots rise together,

29
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.420
the two go together.

30
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.880
Now a trick to watch for.

31
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.020
Our pass rate went from seventy

32
00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.220
to seventy four percent.

33
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.620
If the axis starts at sixty eight, the

34
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.200
new bar looks three times taller.

35
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.540
Bars must start at zero.

36
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.380
Quick check!

37
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.150
You measured a bean plant

38
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.630
every day for two weeks.

39
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.740
Which chart shows how it grew?

40
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:48.770
A line chart.

41
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.700
The days go along the bottom, the

42
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.180
height goes up, and the line shows

43
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.500
the growth over time.

44
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.420
You can even see the days

45
00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.320
when it grew fastest.

46
00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.550
In the app, open Pick the right chart.

47
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.380
Match five questions to their charts,

48
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.580
then drag where the axis starts and

49
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.860
watch a small change look huge.

50
00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:12.730
Your turn.

51
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.980
A poster shows two bars, and

52
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.540
the axis starts at ninety.

53
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:19.620
What should you check first?

54
00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.270
Where the axis starts.

55
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.680
Remember: bars compare groups, lines show

56
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.000
time, pies show parts of a whole, and

57
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.680
scatter shows two measurements.

58
00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.430
And bars start at zero.

59
00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.700
Try it yourself: draw a bar chart of your

60
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.260
family's favourite foods, with a title,

61
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.500
labels and a scale from zero.

62
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.290
Show it to someone and ask what

63
00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.190
they can tell from it.
